from story_common import DISC, A

PART = {

 # DRAFT: no author file for this verse
 1: dict(
  intro=["The Kanakadhāra Stotram, the hymn of Ādi Śaṅkarācārya, the Jagadguru, to Mahalakshmi, opens with a beautiful scene: the graceful sidelong glance of Mahalakshmi brings divine joy to Lord Vishnu.",
         "In this first verse we see how Śaṅkarācārya transforms that glance into poetry. He gives us goosebumps worn like ornaments, a bee moving among flower buds on a dark tamāla tree, and, at the very end, a simple prayer for auspiciousness."],
  story=[
   ("quote","“O Mother Mahalakshmi, your glance is filled with such love and grace that even Lord Vishnu’s body thrills with joy when you look upon Him. Your beauty is like a bee moving among flower buds on a dark tree, and you embody every form of divine glory. May your compassionate glance fall upon me and bring auspiciousness into my life.”"),
   ("sec","The central prayer",["The verse closes with <em>māṅgalya-dā astu mama</em>, “may it bestow auspiciousness on me”, and the one whose glance is meant is <em>maṅgala-devatā</em>, the Goddess of auspiciousness.","The central prayer is therefore not simply for gold. It is for the grace of the Goddess who embodies auspiciousness."]),
   ("sec","The poetry hidden in the first line",["Śaṅkarācārya could have simply written, “Lakshmi looked at Vishnu.” Instead he paints a whole picture.","Imagine it: Vishnu stands serene, dark as a monsoon cloud. Lakshmi turns toward Him. She does not stare; she gives only a gentle glance from the corner of her eye. That glance is filled with so much love that His body is covered in goosebumps of divine bliss, sparkling like jewels.","Śaṅkarācārya sees what ordinary eyes miss."]),
   ("rows","The deeper meaning of the images",[
    ("gem","First image – Pulaka-bhūṣaṇam: goosebumps as ornaments",["Ordinarily we adorn the body with gold, pearls and precious stones. Here Śaṅkarācārya imagines something more beautiful: divine love itself becomes the ornament. Vishnu needs no jewellery to be magnificent; the joy awakened by Lakshmi’s glance adorns Him.","The phrase turns an invisible feeling into a visible image. <em>Pulaka</em> is the goosebumps that rise from intense emotion, and <em>bhūṣaṇa</em> is an ornament."],"The deepest forms of beauty are not always things we can purchase or possess. Sometimes they are the traces of love, devotion and joy that show in a person."),
    ("leaf","Second image – Bhṛṅgāṅganā and the tamāla tree: the bee among flower buds",["The second line extends the painting. Imagine a dark tamāla tree, its branches adorned with delicate flower buds, and a bee moving gracefully among them. Śaṅkarācārya compares Lakshmi’s glance to this bee and Vishnu’s dark form to the tamāla tree.","A bee is drawn to flowers; its movement is delicate and graceful. In the same way her glance brings movement and tenderness to the picture of Vishnu’s serene form.","Traditionally Lakshmi and Vishnu are also seen as inseparable, their relationship expressing divine harmony. It helps to keep the layers apart: the bee and the tree are the verse’s own imagery, while inseparability and harmony are spiritual interpretations we draw from it."],""),
    ("eye","Third image – Apāṅga-līlā: the sidelong glance",["<em>Apāṅga</em> is the corner of the eye, a sidelong glance, and <em>līlā</em> is play or graceful action. Why not simply say that Lakshmi looks at Vishnu? Because the sidelong glance adds delicacy, intimacy and grace.","In classical Indian aesthetics the eyes are powerful instruments of expression: a glance can carry affection, shyness, compassion, longing or delight without a word. In devotional language a deity’s <em>kaṭākṣa</em>, a gracious glance, is a symbol of divine favour."],"The devotee is not merely asking to be seen. The devotee longs to receive compassionate attention."),
   ]),
   ("lessons","Four lessons for life",[
    ("Seek the giver, not merely the gift.","The verse ends with a prayer for auspiciousness, not for a specific quantity of gold. Śaṅkarācārya turns our attention to the Goddess herself. We may ask for material prosperity, but we can also seek the wisdom, integrity and grace that make prosperity meaningful."),
    ("Prosperity is broader than money.","The word <em>vibhūti</em> evokes glory, power and excellence. We can think of prosperity as including material resources along with knowledge, good judgement, courage, generosity and contentment."),
    ("Love is itself an adornment.","<em>Pulaka-bhūṣaṇam</em> reminds us that emotion changes how we see beauty. A person adorned with jewels may be impressive; a person whose face shines with love, joy and compassion has a different kind of beauty. The verse invites us to appreciate both."),
    ("Grace and effort belong together.","The prayer asks for divine auspiciousness. In a practical reading, prayer need not replace effort; it can guide it. We may pray for prosperity while working honestly, managing resources prudently, learning continuously and helping others where we can. Devotion gives direction to effort; effort gives practical expression to our intentions."),
   ],"These wider applications are philosophical reflections inspired by the verse, not a literal list contained in it."),
   ("sec","Reflection for today",["Three questions to carry through the day:",
    "<ul><li><b>What adorns my life?</b> Is it merely what I possess, or also my conduct, relationships and inner qualities?</li><li><b>Where does my attention rest?</b> Am I noticing opportunities for gratitude and goodness, or am I consumed by fear and comparison?</li><li><b>What does auspiciousness mean to me?</b> If prosperity came into my life tomorrow, what would make me use it wisely?</li></ul>",
    "Do not rush to answer; let one question stay with you through the day."]),
   ("chips","Four Sanskrit words to keep",[("Pulaka","goosebumps of intense emotion"),("Apāṅga","the corner of the eye, a sidelong glance"),("Vibhūti","glory, power, excellence"),("Līlā","play, graceful action")]),
   ("note","How to pronounce the key words",["Aṅgam: ang-gam. Hareḥ: ha-reh, ending in a soft breath. Pulaka: pu-la-ka. Bhūṣaṇam: bhoo-sha-nam. Bhṛṅgāṅganeva: bhring-gaan-ga-ne-va. Apāṅgalīlā: a-paan-ga-lee-laa. Māṅgalyadāstu: maang-gal-yaa-stu.","Do not worry about mastering every sound at once; confidence grows gradually."]),
   ("next","Verse 2",["In "+A(2)+" the imagery becomes even more delicate: we meet a great lotus, a bee, and the eyes of the Divine Mother.","Before you go on, hold one question: where does my attention rest? Verse 2 will ask where it keeps returning."]),
  ],
  meditation=["Minute 1, become still: sit comfortably and breathe naturally. Let your attention settle without forcing the mind to become completely silent.",
              "Minutes 2–3, visualise the poetry: imagine Lakshmi’s gentle glance resting upon Vishnu, and His joy shown as a natural radiance, as though His body were adorned by bliss. Recite the verse slowly, resting on apāṅgalīlā.",
              "Minute 4, offer a prayer: say silently, “O Mahalakshmi, may I recognise the blessings already present in my life. May I gain the wisdom to use my resources well and the generosity to share what I can.”",
              "Minute 5, carry the prayer into life: choose one action for today. Help someone, share knowledge, express gratitude, or make a thoughtful financial decision."],
  closing="The bee, the flower buds, the dark tree, the goosebumps and the sidelong glance form one living picture of divine beauty and auspiciousness, and the prayer brings that image close to us: may that auspicious glance bless me." + DISC,
 ),

 2: dict(
  intro=["In "+A(1)+", we witnessed a beautiful scene: the graceful sidelong glance of Mahalakshmi brings divine joy to Lord Vishnu. We explored how Śaṅkarācārya transformed that glance into poetry.",
         "In Verse 2, the imagery becomes even more delicate. We encounter lotus flowers, bees intoxicated by nectar, and the eyes of the Divine Mother.",
         "As before, we will not rush through the Sanskrit. We will decode the words, visualise the scene, understand the spiritual symbolism, and conclude with meditation and reflection."],
  story=[
   ("quote","“May she (the charming Goddess born from the ocean, who repeatedly directs glances towards the face of Murāri, those glances imbued with love and bashfulness, forming a garland like a female bee at a great lotus) grant me Śrī, prosperity and auspiciousness.”"),
   ("sec","A more poetic rendering",["The lovely Goddess, with glances filled with love and tender modesty, looks again and again towards the face of Lord Vishnu.","Her glances move back and forth like a garland of bees hovering around a magnificent lotus.","May that Goddess Lakshmi, born from the ocean, bestow upon me Śrī: prosperity, beauty, good fortune, and auspiciousness."]),
   ("sec","The meaning in simple English",["“O Mother Lakshmi, your eyes are full of love and gentle bashfulness as you look again and again towards Lord Vishnu’s face. Your glances move like bees drawn to a magnificent lotus. O Daughter of the Ocean, may you bless me with Śrī: with prosperity, grace, and auspiciousness.”"]),
   ("sec","Notice a change from Verse 1",["In "+A(1)+", we contemplated the power of a single sidelong glance. In Verse 2, we witness a repeated exchange of glances, full of love and modesty.","The first verse presents the beauty of divine grace. The second brings us closer to the emotional intimacy of the Divine Couple."]),
   ("sec","The poetry hidden in Verse 2",["There is something astonishing here. Śaṅkarācārya could have written: “Lakshmi looks lovingly at Vishnu.” Instead, he gives us an entire scene of glances that come and go, each carrying a delicate emotion.","Imagine Lord Vishnu, calm and magnificent, with the serene beauty of a blue lotus. Lakshmi looks towards His face. Her eyes linger for a moment. Then, perhaps out of modesty, they turn away. And then they return. Again and again.","There is love in the glance, but also <em>trapā</em>, a beautiful reserve, a gentle bashfulness. Śaṅkarācārya does not need to describe a conversation between the Divine Couple. Their eyes speak for them.","And then comes the extraordinary comparison: these repeated glances form a garland of bees around a magnificent lotus. Śaṅkarācārya sees what ordinary eyes miss."]),
   ("table","The emotional genius of the verse",["Image","What it carries"],[
    ["The lotus","The image of beauty, freshness, and attraction."],
    ["The bee","Drawn repeatedly towards the flower, moving around it."],
    ["The glance","Returning again and again, carrying love and bashfulness."],
   ],["The bee’s repeated movement gives us a visual language for the movement of Lakshmi’s eyes.","The lotus attracts the bee. The face of Vishnu draws Lakshmi’s loving attention.","The comparison is not merely decorative. It makes the feeling visible."]),
   ("sec","A subtle distinction",["In the first verse, the bee image evokes Lakshmi’s graceful movement around Vishnu’s dark, tamāla-like form.","In the second, the great lotus becomes the focus of the comparison. The glances are described as a garland, like a bee associated with that lotus.","The imagery develops from one verse to the next, but its emotional centre remains the same: the beauty of the Divine Couple and the grace that Śaṅkarācārya seeks from Lakshmi."]),
   ("rows","The deeper symbolism",[
    ("heart","Insight 1 – Prema-trapā: love and modesty can coexist",["The phrase <em>prema-trapā</em> is one of the most tender expressions in the verse. <em>Prema</em> is love, and <em>trapā</em> is bashfulness or modesty.","The Goddess is not portrayed as distant or impersonal. Her glances express affection, yet retain a graceful reserve.","We should remember that this is a poetic depiction of divine love, not a rule that everyone must express affection through shyness."],"Love does not always need grand declarations. Sometimes tenderness is expressed through restraint, attention, and quiet presence."),
    ("bud","Insight 2 – Mālā dṛśoḥ: the garland of glances",["A garland is made by bringing individual flowers together. Here, Śaṅkarācārya imagines individual glances as a garland. Each glance is a moment of affection; together, they become a continuous expression of love.","There is a lovely idea here: repeated acts of attention can form something beautiful.","In everyday life, a relationship is rarely sustained by one grand gesture. It is sustained by many small ones: listening, remembering, caring, forgiving, and showing up."],""),
    ("lotus","Insight 3 – Mahotpala: why the lotus?",["The lotus occupies a special place in Indian spiritual and literary traditions. It suggests beauty, purity, freshness, and the possibility of flourishing even in muddy water. The lotus is also deeply associated with Lakshmi herself.","In this verse, however, the lotus is part of a specific poetic comparison: the bee is drawn to the great lotus, just as Lakshmi’s glances repeatedly turn towards Vishnu’s face.","We should appreciate both layers, the immediate image and the wider cultural symbolism, without confusing the two."],""),
    ("coin","Insight 4 – Sā me śriyaṁ diśatu: why ask for Śrī?",["The verse ends with a direct prayer: <em>sā me śriyaṁ diśatu</em>, “May she grant me Śrī.” This is a beautiful progression from describing the Goddess to addressing her grace.","The word <em>Śrī</em> can signify prosperity, splendour, beauty, good fortune, and auspiciousness. Here, the prayer is broad enough to invite contemplation of prosperity in its many forms.","For us, that can include material well-being as well as the discernment to use resources wisely, the character to act ethically, and the contentment to recognise what is already good.","That wider application is a reflection inspired by the verse; it should not be mistaken for a literal list of meanings contained in the Sanskrit."],""),
   ]),
   ("sec","The story within the story: Lakshmi as <em>Sāgarasambhavā</em>",["The final compound deserves special attention: <em>sāgarasambhavāyāḥ</em>. It refers to her who was born from the ocean.","In Hindu tradition, Lakshmi is associated with the <em>Samudra Manthana</em>, the churning of the cosmic ocean. During this great event, Lakshmi emerged from the ocean of milk and was welcomed by the gods. The account appears in different forms across the Purāṇic tradition.","Why does Śaṅkarācārya invoke this identity? He is reminding us of the Goddess’s traditional cosmic origin. She is not merely a beautiful figure in an intimate scene with Vishnu; she is the revered Goddess born from the cosmic ocean, associated with auspiciousness and prosperity.","There is a poetic contrast here: the vastness of the ocean and the delicacy of a glance. From a cosmic event, Śaṅkarācārya brings us to a deeply personal prayer: may she grant me Śrī. The universe is vast, yet devotion makes the prayer intimate."]),
   ("sec","Reflection for today",["Here is a question to carry with you: <b>where does your attention return, again and again?</b>","The bee returns to the lotus. Lakshmi’s glance returns to Vishnu. Our own attention also returns to certain things: sometimes to worry, sometimes to ambition, sometimes to gratitude, sometimes to the people we love. Repeated attention gradually shapes our inner world.","Consider three questions:",
    "<ul><li>What occupies my thoughts most often: fear of losing what I have, desire for what I lack, or gratitude for what I possess?</li><li>Do the people important to me receive my attention in small, consistent ways?</li><li>If I seek prosperity, am I also cultivating the qualities that will allow me to use it wisely?</li></ul>",
    "You need not answer every question today. Choose the one that speaks to you most deeply."]),
   ("table","Three Sanskrit treasures to remember",["Word","Meaning","Deeper association"],[
    ["<b>Prema</b> (प्रेम)","Love and affection","The warmth at the heart of the Goddess’s glances."],
    ["<b>Trapā</b> (त्रपा)","Bashfulness and modesty","The delicate reserve that gives the verse its tenderness."],
    ["<b>Śrī</b> (श्री)","Prosperity and auspiciousness","The blessing Śaṅkarācārya seeks from the Goddess born of the ocean."],
   ]),
   ("sec","The essence of Verse 2",["Let us bring the verse together in one final picture.","Lakshmi looks towards Vishnu. Her eyes move away and return, carrying both love and modesty. Śaṅkarācārya sees these repeated glances as a garland of bees around a magnificent lotus. Then he makes his prayer: <em>may the Goddess born from the ocean grant me Śrī</em>.",A(1)+" taught us to contemplate the power of the divine glance. Verse 2 invites us to contemplate the tenderness of that glance: the affection, the repeated attention, and the beauty of a relationship expressed without words."]),
   ("note","A quick pronunciation guide",["Mugdhā: mugdhaa. Muhuḥ: muhur, meaning repeatedly. Murāreḥ: muraareh. Prema-trapā: prema-tra-paa. Gatāgatāni: ga-taa-ga-taa-ni. Madhukarī: ma-dhu-ka-ree. Mahotpale: ma-hot-pa-le. Sāgarasambhavāyāḥ: saa-ga-ra-sam-bha-vaa-yaah.","One small Sanskrit lesson: when words are joined in a verse, their sounds often change slightly. This is called <em>sandhi</em>. We will gradually learn to recognise these joins without making Sanskrit grammar a barrier to enjoying the poetry."]),
   ("next","Verse 3",["A small suggestion for our continuing journey: before moving to "+A(3)+", read "+A(1)+" and Verse 2 aloud once together. Notice how the first verse establishes the divine glance, while the second develops it into a living garland of glances. That continuity is part of the artistry we are learning to recognise."]),
  ],
  meditation=["Minute 1, become still: sit comfortably, breathe gently and let the day’s concerns settle. You do not need to empty your mind; simply allow yourself to be present.",
              "Minutes 2–3, recite the verse: read the Sanskrit slowly, resting on prematrapāpraṇihitāni gatāgatāni. Recall the meaning: glances directed with love and bashfulness, moving back and forth.",
              "Minute 4, visualise the scene: imagine a magnificent lotus, with a bee gently moving around it. Then picture Lakshmi’s loving glance turning towards Vishnu and returning again. Let the image remind you of tenderness, attention and harmony.",
              "Minute 5, offer your prayer: say silently, “O Mother Lakshmi, may my attention be loving, my conduct be graceful, and my understanding of prosperity be wise. May I recognise and nurture the goodness already present in my life.”"],
  closing="What we return to, again and again, becomes an important part of our lives: may we return to wisdom when confused, gratitude when restless, integrity when tempted, and compassion when dealing with others." + DISC,
 ),

 # The author's Verse 3 file has no opening remark, so the intro below is a short bridge from Verses 1 and 2.
 3: dict(
  intro=["In "+A(1)+" the glance of Lakshmi rested on Vishnu as a sidelong glance, and in "+A(2)+" as a garland of repeated glances.",
         "Verse 3 spends most of its lines on how great that glance is, and then makes Śaṅkarācārya’s request in the shortest form possible: half a glance, for a moment."],
  story=[
   ("quote","“May the half-glance of Indirā, capable of bestowing the splendour of Indra’s exalted position and a source of immense joy even to Lord Vishnu, the slayer of Mura, rest lightly upon me for a moment. May that glance, whose beauty is akin to the inner blue of a lotus, bless me.”"),
   ("sec","A more poetic rendering",["O Goddess Indirā, your glance possesses the power to bestow the splendour of the king of the gods. It brings immense joy even to Lord Vishnu, the slayer of Mura.","May just half a glance of yours, beautiful like the deep blue interior of a lotus, rest upon me, even for a single moment.","Here, Murāri refers to Lord Vishnu, the slayer of the demon Mura, and Indirā is another name for Lakshmi. The verse praises the power of her glance and asks for its blessing."]),
   ("sec","The meaning in simple English",["“O Mother Lakshmi, your compassionate glance is so powerful that it can bestow great glory and bring joy even to Lord Vishnu. I ask for so little in comparison: may even a fraction of that divine glance rest upon me for one brief moment and bless my life.”","Notice the contrast: the power described is immense, but the request is remarkably humble."]),
   ("rows","Three important compounds decoded",[
    ("gem","First – Viśvāmarendra-pada-vibhrama-dāna-dakṣam: the splendour of Indra’s station",["The compound brings together the ideas of Indra, his position, its splendour, bestowal, and the ability to grant it.","Meaning: capable of bestowing the splendour and exalted position of Indra, king of the gods.","The idea is not simply that Lakshmi can give ordinary wealth. Her glance is described as powerful enough to confer extraordinary status and glory."],""),
    ("heart","Second – Ānanda-hetur adhikaṁ mura-vidviṣo ’pi: a source of joy even to Vishnu",["Meaning: a source of immense joy even to Lord Vishnu Himself, the enemy of the demon Mura.","This is an especially beautiful thought. The Goddess’s glance is not described only by what it can give to others; it is also associated with the joy of her divine consort."],""),
    ("eye","Third – Īkṣaṇārdham: half a glance",["<em>Īkṣaṇa</em> is a glance and <em>ardha</em> is half. Meaning: half a glance, or a mere fraction of a glance.","This is the emotional centre of the verse. After describing immense divine power, Śaṅkarācārya asks for only a tiny portion of it."],""),
   ]),
   ("sec","The poetry hidden in the verse",["There is something astonishing here. Śaṅkarācārya could have simply written: “Lakshmi is powerful. Please bless me.” Instead, he creates a scene that moves from the grandeur of a heavenly throne to the delicacy of a single glance.","Imagine the scene. First, picture Indra, the king of the gods, surrounded by the magnificence of his heavenly realm. His position represents extraordinary power, prestige, and splendour. Now imagine that the very glory associated with such a position lies within the reach of Lakshmi’s divine grace.","Then Śaṅkarācārya shifts our attention from the heavenly throne to Lord Vishnu. Her glance is a source of profound joy even to Him.","Finally, the grandeur becomes intimate. He asks: may even a fraction of that glance rest upon me for a moment. What a transition! From the throne of Indra to the humble devotee. From cosmic power to a moment of grace.","And then comes the lotus. The Goddess’s glance is described as akin to the inner blue of a lotus, a delicate image of beauty and colour that softens the majesty of the preceding lines.","Śaṅkarācārya sees what ordinary eyes miss: the greatest power can be expressed through the smallest gesture."]),
   ("table","The extraordinary contrast",["Image","What it brings"],[
    ["The grandeur of Indra","Heavenly authority, position, and splendour."],
    ["The joy of Vishnu","The divine consort is associated with profound bliss."],
    ["The lotus-like glance","Beauty expressed through a small, intimate image."],
   ],["The verse brings these three images together to express one prayer: may a little of the Goddess’s extraordinary grace reach the devotee."]),
   ("rows","Five deeper insights",[
    ("scale","Insight 1 – The greatness of the blessing, the humility of the request",["The first line speaks of the power to grant Indra’s exalted position.","Yet Śaṅkarācārya does not demand a kingdom, a throne, or riches. He asks for half a glance, resting upon him for a moment.","This contrast suggests a devotional attitude: the devotee recognises the greatness of the Divine without treating grace as something to be purchased or commanded."],"Approach what you revere with humility, even when your aspirations are great."),
    ("crown","Insight 2 – Even the powerful seek grace",["Indra represents the highest authority among the gods in this imagery. Vishnu represents divine majesty and bliss. Yet the verse presents Lakshmi’s grace as significant to both.","The poetic message is that status and power are not the whole story. Even extraordinary greatness is described in relation to divine grace.","In everyday life, this can remind us that professional success, wealth, or authority should not make us forget gratitude, relationships, or the sources of our good fortune."],""),
    ("drop","Insight 3 – The meaning of “just a moment”",["The phrase <em>kṣaṇam</em> means “for a moment.” Śaṅkarācārya’s request is modest in duration, but immense in its aspiration.","We often imagine that transformation must arrive through some grand event. Yet a moment of insight, a word of wisdom, or an act of compassion can change the direction of a life.","This is a practical reflection inspired by the verse, rather than a literal promise that a brief prayer will automatically change one’s circumstances."],""),
    ("lotus","Insight 4 – The lotus as an image of beauty",["The blue lotus brings a sense of delicacy to a verse otherwise concerned with sovereignty and power.","The lotus is deeply rooted in Indian religious and poetic imagery. It often evokes beauty, purity, freshness, and spiritual significance. Here, the specific comparison is to the inner part of the blue lotus. Its role is to make the Goddess’s glance vivid to the imagination.","Śaṅkarācārya does not merely tell us that the glance is beautiful. He gives us something to see."],""),
    ("path","Insight 5 – Grace and effort",["The verse asks for divine blessing. It does not prescribe a practical programme for success.","We can nevertheless carry its spirit into our lives: pray with sincerity, work with integrity, manage resources prudently, and remain humble when circumstances favour us."],"Grace may inspire our efforts; it should not become an excuse to abandon them."),
   ]),
   ("sec","Reflection for today",["Consider the contrast at the heart of this verse. Śaṅkarācārya invokes the glory of Indra’s throne, yet asks only for a fraction of the Goddess’s glance.","Let us carry three questions into the day:",
    "<ol><li><b>Ambition:</b> Am I pursuing success while also cultivating the character needed to handle it?</li><li><b>Humility:</b> When things go well, do I remember the people, opportunities, and circumstances that helped me?</li><li><b>Grace:</b> Can I be grateful for what I already possess while continuing to work towards what I hope to achieve?</li></ol>",
    "Choose one question to contemplate quietly."]),
   ("table","Three Sanskrit treasures",["Word","Meaning","Deeper association"],[
    ["<b>Ānanda</b> (आनन्द)","Bliss","A deep joy that goes beyond ordinary pleasure."],
    ["<b>Īkṣaṇa</b> (ईक्षण)","A glance","A look or act of seeing; in this verse, the object of the devotee’s prayer."],
    ["<b>Dakṣa</b> (दक्ष)","Capable or skilful","In the verse, it describes the Goddess’s capacity to bestow great glory."],
   ]),
   ("sec","The essence of Verse 3",["Let us gather the verse into one final image.","A heavenly throne represents extraordinary power. Lord Vishnu represents divine majesty and bliss. The blue lotus gives the Goddess’s glance a delicate, vivid beauty.","Then Śaṅkarācārya turns towards his own prayer: <em>may even half a glance rest upon me for a moment</em>.","The heart of the verse is not merely the greatness of what Lakshmi can bestow. It is the humility with which Śaṅkarācārya seeks her grace."]),
   ("note","Pronunciation guide",["Viśvāmarendra: vish-vaa-ma-ren-dra. Vibhrama: vi-bhra-ma. Dakṣam: dak-sham. Mura-vidviṣaḥ: mu-ra-vid-vi-shah. Īṣan niṣīdatu: ee-shan ni-shee-da-tu. Īkṣaṇārdham: eek-sha-naar-dham. Indīvarodara: in-dee-va-ro-da-ra.","Read the verse slowly, giving each line time to settle. Our goal is not speed; it is understanding."]),
   # The author's Verse 3 file has no look-ahead; this one is drawn from the verified Verse 4 data in build.py.
   ("next","Verse 4",["Next is "+A(4)+", <em>āmīlitākṣam adhigamya mudā mukundam…</em>","Having asked for half a glance, we now turn to the eye itself: the eye of Lakshmi, half-closed and unblinking, resting with joy on Mukunda, the root of bliss."]),
  ],
  meditation=["Minute 1, become still: sit comfortably and breathe naturally. Allow the day’s concerns to settle, and do not force the mind to become empty.",
              "Minutes 2–3, recite the verse: read the Sanskrit slowly, resting on īṣan niṣīdatu mayi kṣaṇam īkṣaṇārdham. Let its meaning settle: may even a fraction of the glance rest upon me for a moment.",
              "Minute 4, visualise the lotus: imagine a blue lotus in still water, and let it represent the beauty and serenity of the Goddess’s glance. You do not need to force a vision; simply contemplate the image.",
              "Minute 5, offer a prayer: say silently, “O Mother Lakshmi, may I receive the wisdom to recognise my blessings, the humility to remain grounded, and the discernment to use my resources for good.”"],
  closing="Aspire greatly, remain humble, and remember that prosperity is most meaningful when accompanied by wisdom and gratitude." + DISC,
 ),

}
